<Page title="Claims, capacity, and collateral — Alexander Campos" description="Working research on the distance between financial commitments to compute, usable infrastructure, and cash earned from useful work.">
  <article class="research">
    <h1>Claims, capacity, and collateral</h1>
    <p class="lede">A contract for compute is a claim on work that still has to happen.</p>
    <p>
      I’m studying how that claim becomes working capacity, useful output, and
      eventually cash. Those transitions take different amounts of time and
      depend on different people. Treating them as the same thing can hide
      both a bad investment and an opportunity to build something valuable.
    </p>
    <p>
      The question that started the paper was financial: are promises of future
      compute being valued as though the capacity is already running and earning?
      It has become a broader question about what happens when intelligence gets
      cheaper faster than the physical and institutional systems around it can change.
    </p>
    <p>
      My broader thesis is that, as intelligence becomes more abundant,
      economic power depends increasingly on the means of converting it into
      consequential outcomes: capital, infrastructure, data, permissions,
      distribution, and institutions. I’m interested in who controls that
      conversion, who captures the value, who bears the risks, and what choices
      remain for the people inside it. I want to build useful systems at those
      points as well as study them.
    </p>

    <h2>Follow the conversion</h2>
    <p>
      I separate a project into six states: announced, contracted, financed,
      energized, billable, and producing cash after its obligations. Each state
      needs its own date and evidence. A customer prepayment can arrive before
      the service exists. A building can have power without having a paying workload.
      A busy cluster can still earn too little to cover its financing and replacement costs.
    </p>
    <p>
      That makes the research specific. How long does each transition take?
      What still has to be spent? Can the customer cancel? Who bears a delay?
      What capacity is actually being counted? The next step is a dated,
      project-level record that can answer those questions consistently.
    </p>

    <h2>Find what stays scarce</h2>
    <p>
      If a model becomes cheaper, the value may move to something needed around
      it: firm power, a usable site, a customer relationship, reliable operating
      context, or permission to act inside an organization. The opportunity is
      to find which constraint is binding in a particular place and whether
      solving it produces something someone will pay for.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two sites advertised with the same capacity can have very different
      economics. One may have firm utility service and a customer; the other
      may still need a substation. A national average price for power tells
      you very little about that difference.
    </p>
    <p>
      I’m interested in what existing infrastructure can be made useful again.
      The questions are practical: service rights, equipment, cooling,
      reliability, remaining cost, and who needs the output. Local effects on
      water, power bills, and land use belong in that calculation too.
    </p>

    <h2>Count completed work</h2>
    <p>
      In software, the useful unit is a completed task at an acceptable quality,
      latency, and total cost. Tokens alone leave out retries, human review,
      integration work, and the consequences of being wrong. I run into a
      smaller version of that problem in my own model routing: a cheap request
      can be an expensive way to finish a job.
    </p>
    <p>
      Better models can make a service more useful while competition lowers
      the price it can charge. Whether the owner of the underlying hardware
      benefits depends on the costs, contracts, utilization, and replacement
      schedule. The technology’s success and the return on a particular asset
      need separate explanations.
    </p>

    <h2>Keep the counterargument in view</h2>
    <p>
      Demand could grow quickly enough to absorb the capacity. Efficiency could
      improve faster than prices fall. Contracts could protect owners better
      than a headline suggests. A project with heavy construction spending
      could be a sound investment, and a well-run facility could still be
      overpriced. The method has to be able to find all of those cases.
    </p>
    <p>
      Before calling a mispricing, I want to compare what a price assumes about
      delivery and earnings with what the underlying evidence supports. Then
      test whether the difference survives financing costs, missing data,
      and a serious alternative explanation.
    </p>

    <h2>Who gets to decide</h2>
    <p>
      The other thread is agency. Cheap intelligence can expand what people
      are able to do. That benefit is limited if they cannot inspect the
      records used to judge them, change the tools they rely on, or leave a
      provider without losing their working context. I’m interested in both
      the business that captures value and the choices left to the person using it.
    </p>
    <p>
      That’s the connection to <a href="/projects/forgeframe">my software work</a>.
      At a different scale, I’m asking what someone can control, what they’re
      depending on, and how much it costs to change course.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading and method</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="https://www.iea.org/reports/energy-and-ai">IEA’s Energy and AI</a>
      is a starting point for the physical system. Berkeley Lab’s
      <a href="https://emp.lbl.gov/queues">Queued Up</a> provides interconnection
      data for generation and storage. Its queue statistics describe supply
      projects; they cannot simply be applied to data-center load requests.
    </p>
    <p>
      For financial claims, I work from filings, contracts, and their definitions,
      keeping reported figures separate from estimates and scenarios. I’m
      developing the project-level measurements; I don’t yet have an original
      dataset or a validated trading result to publish here.
    </p>
